(Loop expansion turns one action into a loop: `<action> each x from
collection` runs the action once per element, and the same shape composes
into a grid of several `each` clauses, or takes a `but if` branch inside
the loop. See LANGUAGE.md, "Loop Expansion".)

To 'announce a class' with a text called craft.
  Print "This week: {craft}.".

a list called crafts is ["pottery", "weaving", "smithing"].

(A single each clause turns one call into one per item)
'announce a class' of each craft from crafts.

Print "".

To 'pair a tool with a material' with a text called tool and a text called material.
  Print "{tool} works {material}".

a list called tools is ["chisel", "loom"].
a list called materials is ["clay", "wool"].

(Two each clauses chain into a grid: the leftmost clause is the outer
 loop, so every tool gets a turn with every material)
'pair a tool with a material' of each tool from tools and each material from materials.

Print "".

To 'schedule a class' with a text called weekday and a text called craft.
  Print "{weekday}: {craft} class".

a list called weekdays is ["Monday", "Wednesday"].

(A `but if` branch attaches to the innermost call of a grid, so its
 condition can read every loop variable at once - here, flagging the one
 slot that is already booked)
'schedule a class' of each weekday from weekdays and each craft from crafts,
  but if weekday is "Wednesday" and craft is "smithing" print "{weekday}: smithing is full, try next week".

Print "Done!".

(Run output, 16 lines: three "This week: ..." lines, a blank, four
 "... works ..." pairings, a blank, six scheduled slots with
 Wednesday/smithing overridden, then "Done!")
